{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1407266-how-are-ars-magica-and-mage-the-ascension-related","id":1407266,"title":"How are Ars Magica and Mage the Ascension related?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-08-10T04:17:32-07:00","id":1407266,"title":"How are Ars Magica and Mage the Ascension related?","content":"Are they the same universe?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T05:29:45-07:00","id":1407277,"title":"","content":"They used to be, when the World of Darkness was first created. Then Ars Magica ended up with a different owner, and the official ties between the two disappeared.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T05:45:44-07:00","id":1407279,"title":"","content":"Ars Magica 3rd edition (and possibly the end of 2nd and beginning of 4th) was produced by <i>White Wolf</i>. It was all about the <i>Order of Hermes</i>, and was then the origin of some of the Mage line. When Atlas Games took over production (with the 4th Ed core book), the timeline link was removed, but some of the IP was still used in both - especially around names, locations and some of the history.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26145-babi_gog","name":"babi_gog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26145_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"94"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T05:55:58-07:00","id":1407281,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>babi_gog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1407266-how-are-ars-magica-and-mage-the-ascension-related?p=1407279#post1407279\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ars Magica 3rd edition (and possibly the end of 2nd and beginning of 4th) was produced by <i>White Wolf</i>. It was all about the <i>Order of Hermes</i>, and was then the origin of some of the Mage line. When Atlas Games took over production (with the 4th Ed core book), the timeline link was removed, but some of the IP was still used in both - especially around names, locations and some of the history.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T07:45:35-07:00","id":1407292,"title":"","content":"Dark Ages Mage has basically replaced early Ars Magica as the prequel for Mage the Ascension, though it's still possible to incorporate the fluff from the prior editions. As mentioned by others, 4th Edition onward has deviated from the timeline, but anything prior is still applicable in the broad strokes, I think.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T08:09:49-07:00","id":1407296,"title":"","content":"What is the historical event that causes Fourth Edition Ars Magica to split from the Dark Ages WoD timeline? (Did I say that right?)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T08:32:12-07:00","id":1407301,"title":"","content":"I've never played it, so someone else is probably better fit to explain. As far as I understand, the biggest difference is that House Tremere still exists as a House, and the vampires have been removed from play. I think there are other, smaller things too. <br /> <br /> I suppose it doesn't necessarily mean later edition Ars Magica CAN'T be a prequel. It predates the fall of Mistridge after all, and one could perhaps go with the idea that the Mages of Tremere only THOUGHT they got rid of the vampires only to lose the long game. But it's no longer considered directly linked the way 3rd Edition specifically was, and 1st and 2nd were heavily implied to be.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T09:02:02-07:00","id":1407308,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Penelope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1407266-how-are-ars-magica-and-mage-the-ascension-related?p=1407296#post1407296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What is the historical event that causes Fourth Edition Ars Magica to split from the Dark Ages WoD timeline? (Did I say that right?)</div> </div> </div> </div>Hard to say. Ars Magica's version of mystic Europe completely lacks things like Cainite vampires or Garou werewolves, as well as any of the groups that would form the foundations for the Technocracy, Verbena, Celestial Chorus or so forth. So anything that would've involved any of those groups and their influences never happen. And the founder of House Tremere has apparently been dead for a number of years. <br /> Also, metaphysics seems to be different, with four distinct realms of power (Magic, Divine, Infernal and Faeire, IIRC) that all the mystical stuff derives from. And, again IIRC, the further one gets from Europe, the weirder things get as the world apparently tends to conform to what medieval Europeans thought the rest of the world was like rather than what it actually was. (There's a 2nd ed Ars Magica book on sub-Saharan Africa called South of the Sun which, in addition to the Kingdom of Prestor John, contains a multitude of period weirdness.)<br /> <br /> I've mostly used the various sourcebooks as idea fodder for the Order of Hermes (the various books on the Houses help fill in details about the original ones, especially the ones that got stuck into Ex Misc) and for period details about medieval Europe (the book on Mythic Judaism is one of my personal favorites, but the 5th edition regional books all have really good maps that I've found insanely useful).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T09:29:05-07:00","id":1407312,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Penelope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1407266-how-are-ars-magica-and-mage-the-ascension-related?p=1407296#post1407296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What is the historical event that causes Fourth Edition Ars Magica to split from the Dark Ages WoD timeline? (Did I say that right?)</div> </div> </div> </div>There isn't really an event that splits them, before 3rd ed Ars Magica there was no link - in 3rd ed a tentative link through the OoH was established (this was also linked with the firm becoming part of White Wolf) - then in 4th ed the link was no longer there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26145-babi_gog","name":"babi_gog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26145_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"94"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T10:09:06-07:00","id":1407321,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1407266-how-are-ars-magica-and-mage-the-ascension-related?p=1407308#post1407308\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Hard to say. Ars Magica's version of mystic Europe completely lacks things like Cainite vampires or Garou werewolves, as well as any of the groups that would form the foundations for the Technocracy, Verbena, Celestial Chorus or so forth. So anything that would've involved any of those groups and their influences never happen. And the founder of House Tremere has apparently been dead for a number of years. <br /> Also, metaphysics seems to be different, with four distinct realms of power (Magic, Divine, Infernal and Faeire, IIRC) that all the mystical stuff derives from. And, again IIRC, the further one gets from Europe, the weirder things get as the world apparently tends to conform to what medieval Europeans thought the rest of the world was like rather than what it actually was. (There's a 2nd ed Ars Magica book on sub-Saharan Africa called South of the Sun which, in addition to the Kingdom of Prestor John, contains a multitude of period weirdness.)<br /> <br /> I've mostly used the various sourcebooks as idea fodder for the Order of Hermes (the various books on the Houses help fill in details about the original ones, especially the ones that got stuck into Ex Misc) and for period details about medieval Europe (the book on Mythic Judaism is one of my personal favorites, but the 5th edition regional books all have really good maps that I've found insanely useful).</div> </div> </div> </div>That\u2019s great! Thanks \ud83d\ude0a. I\u2019m gonna buy a bunch of old Ars Magica Tribunal books and see if I can scavenge some useful stuff out of them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T11:35:36-07:00","id":1407336,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Penelope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1407266-how-are-ars-magica-and-mage-the-ascension-related?p=1407321#post1407321\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That\u2019s great! Thanks \ud83d\ude0a. I\u2019m gonna buy a bunch of old Ars Magica Tribunal books and see if I can scavenge some useful stuff out of them.</div> </div> </div> </div>IIRC, the list of Tribunal books are as follows, <br /> <br /> From 5th edition:<br /> <br /> <b>Guardians of the Forests: The Rhine Tribunal</b> - A large part of Germany, as well as the Low Countries.<br /> <b>The Lion &amp; The Lily: The Normandy Tribunal</b> - Northern and central France.<br /> <b>Faith &amp; Flame: The Provencal Tribunal</b> - Southern France.<br /> <b>Against the Dark: The Transylvanian Tribunal</b> - Most of what is now Romania, Hungary, the Balkans, Slovakia and Bulgaria. (It's dominated by the Tremere, and can help flesh out some of the real world regional stuff from the early Transylvania Chronicles and House of Tremere.)<br /> <b>The Contested Isle: The Hibernian Tribunal</b> - Ireland<br /> <b>The Sundered Eagle: The Theban Tribunal</b> - Greece and Asia Minor<br /> <br /> And a trio of books that are set up like Tribunal books but technically aren't, covering the Middle East.<br /> <br /> <b>Cradle and The Crescent</b> - About Mesopotamia, Arabia and Persia. (I don't have this one, but it's on my list of ones to acquire, as it apparently includes a rival magical group, The Order of Solomon, and a description of Mythic Zoroastrianism.) <br /> <b>Lands of the Nile</b> - About Egypt, Nubia and Ethiopia. <br /> <b>Between Sand &amp; Sea: Mythic Africa</b> - About North Africa west of Egypt. <br /> <br /> From 4th edition:<br /> <br /> <b>Heirs to Merlin: The Stonehenge Tribunal</b> - England and Wales. (Obviously, this would be mostly superseded by the stuff in Dark Ages: Britain, but there's a number of interesting ideas to mine, and the maps are pretty good.)<br /> <b>Sanctuary of Ice: The Greater Alpine Tribunal</b> - The Alps of central Europe and Bohemia. <br /> <b>The Dragon and the Bear: The Novgorod Tribunal </b>- Poland and Russia.<br /> <b>Blood and Sand: The Levant Tribunal</b> - The Holy Land.<br /> <br /> And again, a pair of books about an area that doesn't actually have tribunals - <b>Ultima Thule: Mythic Scandinavia</b> and <b>Land of Fire and Ice: Mythic Iceland</b> - which, if you want to do stuff with the Valdaermen, are probably pretty useful. <br /> <br /> And then Third Edition, which was the one White Wolf did a big chunk of. I should note that these seem to have a weird fixation on Infernalism, which I guess was just a thing with the company back in the early 90s, as its very prevalent in a lot of 1st ed WoD stuff as well. However, it moved to Atlas near the end of this edition, so the final Tribunal book doesn't seem to share this infatuation. And some of the stuff in this apparently isn't canon anymore, but if you're just using them as idea fodder for Dark Ages, that doesn't really matter. <br /> <br /> <b>Tribunals of Hermes: Iberia</b><br /> <b>Tribunals of Hermes: Rome</b> - which is the majority of Italy.<br /> <b>Lion of the North: The Loch Leglean Tribunal</b> - Scotland.<br /> <br /> And as I mentioned, the 2nd edition book, <b>South of the Sun</b>, which is a <i>deeply</i> weird take on Sub-Saharan Africa. <br /> <br /> <br /> In addition, fifth edition has a trio of Houses of Hermes books (True Lineages, Societates, and Mystery Cults) and a four book set about medieval society (Lords of Men, The Church, City &amp; Guild, and Art &amp; Academe). <br /> <br /> Forth edition's two standouts, for my uses at least, are Kabbalah: Mythic Judaism and the Medieval Bestiary Revised. <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T12:28:58-07:00","id":1407352,"title":"","content":"The various books on the Houses of Hermes can be useful for Mage if you have Order of Hermes character concepts for the houses in Ars Magica that still exist in the Mage universe. I have the Guardians of the Forest book in PDF and just scored a copy of Land of Fire and Ice in paper (it's hardcover) for my Verbena player through my local Halfprice Books.<br /> <br /> I have owned in paper and in PDF the version with Jonathan Tweet as the author and published by Wizards of the Coast. Yep, the big Magic card makers got an Ars Magica book in there right between 3rd and 4th edition. Speaking of the 4th edition of Ars Magica, it's free on the Warehouse 23 store, probably because Steve Jackson Games has carried PDFs for Atlas Games products for a while and never removed the 4e book that was being given out for free during the transition to Ars Magica 5e.<br /> <br /> While there's not a ton of detail on all the locations and houses and the rules will differ a little, the free 4e PDF should give you an idea of where Ars Magica is going. If you like more and like PDF, Steve Jackson Games sells several editions worth of Ars Magica through Warehouse 23. . .<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://www.warehouse23.com/products?utf8=%E2%9C%93&amp;search%5Bprice_range_any%5D%5B%5D=Free_&amp;keywords=ars+magica\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.warehouse23.com/products?utf8=%E2%9C%93&amp;search%5Bprice_range_any%5D%5B%5D=Free_&amp;keywords=ars+magica\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.warehouse23.com/products?utf8=%E2%9C%93&amp;search%5Bprice_range_any%5D%5B%5D=Free_&amp;keywords=ars+magica\" target=\"_blank\"> http://www.warehouse23.com/products?utf8=%E2%9C%93&amp;search%5Bprice_range_any%5D%5B%5D=Free_&amp;keywords=ars+magica </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T12:33:35-07:00","id":1407354,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1407266-how-are-ars-magica-and-mage-the-ascension-related?p=1407336#post1407336\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> IIRC, the list of Tribunal books are as follows, <br /> <br /> From 5th edition:<br /> <br /> <b>Guardians of the Forests: The Rhine Tribunal</b> - A large part of Germany, as well as the Low Countries.<br /> <b>The Lion &amp; The Lily: The Normandy Tribunal</b> - Northern and central France.<br /> <b>Faith &amp; Flame: The Provencal Tribunal</b> - Southern France.<br /> <b>Against the Dark: The Transylvanian Tribunal</b> - Most of what is now Romania, Hungary, the Balkans, Slovakia and Bulgaria. (It's dominated by the Tremere, and can help flesh out some of the real world regional stuff from the early Transylvania Chronicles and House of Tremere.)<br /> <b>The Contested Isle: The Hibernian Tribunal</b> - Ireland<br /> <b>The Sundered Eagle: The Theban Tribunal</b> - Greece and Asia Minor<br /> <br /> And a trio of books that are set up like Tribunal books but technically aren't, covering the Middle East.<br /> <br /> <b>Cradle and The Crescent</b> - About Mesopotamia, Arabia and Persia. (I don't have this one, but it's on my list of ones to acquire, as it apparently includes a rival magical group, The Order of Solomon, and a description of Mythic Zoroastrianism.) <br /> <b>Lands of the Nile</b> - About Egypt, Nubia and Ethiopia. <br /> <b>Between Sand &amp; Sea: Mythic Africa</b> - About North Africa west of Egypt. <br /> <br /> From 4th edition:<br /> <br /> <b>Heirs to Merlin: The Stonehenge Tribunal</b> - England and Wales. (Obviously, this would be mostly superseded by the stuff in Dark Ages: Britain, but there's a number of interesting ideas to mine, and the maps are pretty good.)<br /> <b>Sanctuary of Ice: The Greater Alpine Tribunal</b> - The Alps of central Europe and Bohemia. <br /> <b>The Dragon and the Bear: The Novgorod Tribunal </b>- Poland and Russia.<br /> <b>Blood and Sand: The Levant Tribunal</b> - The Holy Land.<br /> <br /> And again, a pair of books about an area that doesn't actually have tribunals - <b>Ultima Thule: Mythic Scandinavia</b> and <b>Land of Fire and Ice: Mythic Iceland</b> - which, if you want to do stuff with the Valdaermen, are probably pretty useful. <br /> <br /> And then Third Edition, which was the one White Wolf did a big chunk of. I should note that these seem to have a weird fixation on Infernalism, which I guess was just a thing with the company back in the early 90s, as its very prevalent in a lot of 1st ed WoD stuff as well. However, it moved to Atlas near the end of this edition, so the final Tribunal book doesn't seem to share this infatuation. And some of the stuff in this apparently isn't canon anymore, but if you're just using them as idea fodder for Dark Ages, that doesn't really matter. <br /> <br /> <b>Tribunals of Hermes: Iberia</b><br /> <b>Tribunals of Hermes: Rome</b> - which is the majority of Italy.<br /> <b>Lion of the North: The Loch Leglean Tribunal</b> - Scotland.<br /> <br /> And as I mentioned, the 2nd edition book, <b>South of the Sun</b>, which is a <i>deeply</i> weird take on Sub-Saharan Africa. <br /> <br /> <br /> In addition, fifth edition has a trio of Houses of Hermes books (True Lineages, Societates, and Mystery Cults) and a four book set about medieval society (Lords of Men, The Church, City &amp; Guild, and Art &amp; Academe). <br /> <br /> Forth edition's two standouts, for my uses at least, are Kabbalah: Mythic Judaism and the Medieval Bestiary Revised. <br /> <br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>This is great. Thank you. I\u2019m definitely gonna buy all the Tribunal books (obviously not all at once of course) and probably the books about Africa, the Middle East and the Nordic lands. And maybe Mythic Judaism. Probably not South of the Sun though; I heard it\u2019s really weird and doesn\u2019t go with the tone of the later books.<br /> <br /> And OMG I know! I read Iberia and Rome and they were basically obsessed with Infernalism. I\u2019m glad the later books aren\u2019t like that.<br /> <br /> Thanks again.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T14:41:57-07:00","id":1407379,"title":"","content":"For some of the Ars Magica info and so forth <a href=\"https://www.redcap.org/page/Main_Page\" target=\"_blank\">Project Redcap</a> is worth a look","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26145-babi_gog","name":"babi_gog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26145_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"94"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T20:27:41-07:00","id":1407434,"title":"","content":"Mage the Ascension was originally designed to be Ars Magica but set in the future where the various wizards unifying their magic to make the Spheres. After the split this obviously ended.<br /> Also fun fact: the video game Dominions was originally about playing Mage the Ascension characters who were trying to become gods before it also became its own thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1VN49Y7tH2ZV0l7Hx65RWX1ez-Q9Lst-8ZTyTJeCTpxM/\" target=\"_blank\">Sorcerer 20th</a> homebrew and my update to <a href=\"http://immortalthegathering.blogspot.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Highlander: the Gathering</a> for 20th Anniversary edition.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2414-theoutlander523","name":"theoutlander523","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2414_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"238"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-10T23:31:36-07:00","id":1407452,"title":"","content":"I think there is a strong argument to say that Ars Magica was the game that first coined the notion of being a \u201cstorytelling\u201d game, along with the whole indie/narrative game schtick back in the day. It was written by Mark Rein-Hagen and Jonathon Tweet and was independently published under the \u2018Lion Rampart\u2019 moniker. It also innovated things like troupe style play and had a magic system with a structured set of rules, but with freeform applications. It had a different core system to the WoD games, using singular D10s rather than dice pools, but some of the terminology and modes of play filtered through.<br /> <br /> The Houses of Hermes was a splat-book that established 12 Houses that your wizards could join up with, in a manner similar to the way WoD games structure themselves with Clans/Tribes/Traditions, etc. The game centred around a fictional wizardly order - The Order of Hermes - which was then used in Mage: The Ascension some time later and included House Tremere which later became Clan Tremere in Vampire: The Masquerade. The 1st edition (c. 1987) was actually setting less, albeit with an implied medieval set up, but the \u2018Mythic Europe\u2019 setting became developed in the 2nd edition (1989) onwards, as was the Order of Hermes and the various Houses and lore. Mark Rein-Hagen\u2019s intent was to further develop the setting by producing multiple games in it - as was done later in his WoD games - while Jonathon Tweet had also mooted designing a modern magic type of game in the near future. Neither quite came to fruition as Rein-Hagen became involved with White Wolf games, and started to work on Vampire: The Masquerade as his next project, while Jonathon Tweet took a brief hiatus from game design, although he eventually went on to design Over The Edge for Atlas Games, which could be seen as his own \u2018modern magic\u2019 game.<br /> <br /> When Rein-Hagen joined White Wolf, alongside the Weick brothers and others who were big fans, he essentially took Ars Magica with him and a third edition was released about 1992, just after Vampire: The Masquerade was proving a big hit and in its 2nd edition release. One of the notable shifts in the AM 3rd edition, apart from the appreciable darkening of tone, was the introduction of the 'Realm of Reason\u2019 and the fledgling 'Order of Reason\u2019. Some fans of Ars Magica hated that inclusion as they felt it undermined the point of the game being set in a medieval paradigm, but it was being set up as a sort of historical setting for the WoD as a whole, including the advertised upcoming Mage game which had yet to be released. It is worth noting that Mage: The Ascension, written by the late Stewart Weick, was the only game in the original WoD series that was not directly created and developed by Mark Rein-Hagen.<br /> <br /> There were a couple of regional splat books and some other supplements released by White Wolf for Ars Magica, but they had dropped it as a title by the mid-1990s and this was then picked up briefly by Wizards of the Coast (with Jonathon Tweet employed as their lead RPG designer) who also decided to drop it not long after. Eventually, Atlas Games who had already published Jonathon Tweet\u2019s Over The Edge, and would go on to pick up and develop other similar games to Mage like Feng Shui and Unknown Armies, picked up and released a 4th edition of Ars Magica with certain connections to the WoD, like the Order of Reason, removed from it. A 5th edition was published about 2003 and between both these editions they chugged out a load of finely researched and detailed supplements that make up the bulk of their line today.<br /> <br /> Mage: The Ascension also ended up with a load of supplements, that developed their own setting in its own direction, and by the time of the Revised edition\u2019s final run, it had two historical settings of its own: Mage: The Sorcerer\u2019s Crusade and latterly the Dark Ages: Mage supplement. These largely completed the severance of the connection between the Ars Magica and Mage lines.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2517,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2517-trippy","user":"Trippy","date":". 08-21-2020, 05:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2517-trippy","name":"Trippy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2517_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1018"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-11T04:57:20-07:00","id":1407485,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>theoutlander523</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1407266-how-are-ars-magica-and-mage-the-ascension-related?p=1407434#post1407434\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mage the Ascension was originally designed to be Ars Magica but set in the future where the various wizards unifying their magic to make the Spheres. After the split this obviously ended.<br /> Also fun fact: the video game Dominions was originally about playing Mage the Ascension characters who were trying to become gods before it also became its own thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>That sounds like a cool idea. I wish they hadn\u2019t split.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-14T15:12:21-07:00","id":1408252,"title":"","content":"I never played Ars Magica, but I've read some of it for learning purposes and I though there were always some elements* that were nifty. I like the magic system for example, just as I like the Arcana of Mage the Awakening far more than Spheres (or DA:M's foundations and pillars lol.) My view of mage has always seen there being 'multiple potential paths to the same endpoint' with sphere magic perhaps being the most 'pure' or closest to original intent (and thus being more universal and easy to adapt to other factions unlike MtA's Arcana or Foundation/Pillars.)<br /> <br /> *I've sometimes toyed with the idea of the Ars Magica magic system being a good way to introduce some style of 'name'-based magic, sort of like the MtR 'Nomenclature' - or as a companion to DtF's lores and evocations (Lores were there 'built in' mechanisms for manipulating reality - to borrow a Matrix-esque analogy they were purpose built programs anyone could use to manipulate the system. The Ars Magica system would be more akin to directly hacking or programming... something that is more flexible and allows for more stuff to be done compared to the more limited Lores.. but also taking longer to do and with greater risks (more changes for errors to occur in the program, which have the potential to be catastrophic.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25533-mister_dunpeal","name":"Mister_Dunpeal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"308"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-20T03:06:21-07:00","id":1409318,"title":"","content":"I have been playing Ars Magica for decades and I have even written for the game. I was excited to say a whole bunch of stuff, but others beat me too it, and they are mostly accurate.<br /> <br /> Ars Magica was created by Jonathan Tweet, the same guy who engineered D&amp;D 3rd edition, and it is indeed the very first of its kind as far as a troupe style story focused game with flexible improvised magic along with magic formula. Mark Rein*Hagen joined in with the second edition of that game, published by Lion Rampart, and it begat a strong cult following. Jon Nephew was a major player at Lion Rampart, and wrote some for that edition (as well as a few becmi D&amp;D books). Tweet and Rein*Hagen started sowing the seed for the 0WoD. References to Tremere magi (and some Boniagus) experimenting with the undead can be found in 2nd ed books such as Order of Hermes, Covenants, and others.<br /> <br /> White Wolf Magazine merges with Lion Rampart, and proceeds to cannibalize Ars Magica to create Vampire. I mean, I do admire aspects of 3rd edition Ars, but WW did a lot of mucking up with secret infernal plots everywhere. But a lot of 3rd edition produced the core of that games future, as well as the core for Mage. We have the introduction of Mistridge and Grimgroth.<br /> <br /> Ars Magica already had a cult of fans. They *hated* third edition. There were massive flame wars on the primitive internet of the day. I was there. Second became known as the One True Edition, and to this day, the Ars Magica community on the Atlas Forums and Facebook hate 3rd edition and Tremere vampires with a passion. They hate the entire world of darkness, and they are split on Mage. Most hate it. And most of them do not know White Wolf is dead.<br /> <br /> Jon Nephew, who used to write for the game, formed Atlas Games and bought the game from White Wolf and ran hard with it. The fans saw fourth edition as reclaiming thier game. The Tremere were expunged, the infernal references toned down, and a lot of work went into making Ars and Mythic Europe its own special thing. That version retained some terminology fron 2nd and 3rd that is found in oWoD games, such as the Talent/Skill/Knowledge categories and the names of Virtues and Flaws. It is not a dice pool game. Straight d10 for everything. The core mechanics of Ars Magica formed the basis of the re engineering of d&amp;d3.<br /> <br /> Then we get to fifth edition Ars Magica, under David Chart as line editor. The edition I wrote for, the version that sold twice as many books as all versions before it and lasted longer than all of them together. They ended production a few years ago and the fan base is still powerful.In 5th edition, there never were any Tremere Vampires. Flambeau got retconned (one of the reasons I got into writing for the game is because I liked Flambeau the way it was and wanted to guide it back). The T/S/K division was taken out, and Virtues/Flaws became a matter of Major/Minor instead of a direct point value. Here, Ars Magica truly grew into its own. The setting, the mechanics, the philosophy, by far the best version yet.<br /> <br /> It is a very, very, very crunchy game. It makes you feel like a wizard with the formulas. And it is rewarding. It is a game for people who feel they did not get enough homework in school. It is much more troupe focused and story driven than any other game, yet it is crunchier and more precise than Gygax D&amp;D. It is not anything like mage. It does not feel the same, play the same, or have the same tone. If you try to adapt pieces of it to mage, you will wind up giving up mage and playing Ars Magica instead.It does everything you wish mage would do.<br /> <br /> Obviously I have a bias <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://forum.atlas-games.com/c/play-by-post-campaigns/ars-magica-5th-edition-light-of-andorra/32\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.atlas-games.com/uploads/default/original/1X/ceadc4b228e55aec49e787add129dcbe5f6a8c70.png\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://forum.atlas-games.com/c/play-by-post-campaigns/ars-magica-5th-edition-light-of-andorra/32\" target=\"_blank\"> Ars Magica 5th Edition: Light of Andorra </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://forum.atlas-games.com/c/play-by-post-campaigns/ars-magica-5th-edition-light-of-andorra/32\" target=\"_blank\"> https://forum.atlas-games.com/c/play-by-post-campaigns/ars-magica-5th-edition-light-of-andorra/32 </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> Adventure! Glory! Legend and Mystery! This is the bread and butter of the magi of Andorra. But power and prosperity are but mere distractions, and the covenant has some difficult choices and challenges ahead. </div> </div> </div><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25651-marko-markoko","name":"Marko Markoko","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25651_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"123"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-20T03:43:48-07:00","id":1409320,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_con